    "reply": "The KRAS gene yields a product functioning as a critical regulatory enzyme within the cell [11: \"encodes a protein that is a member of the small GTPase superfamily\"]. This protein normally controls cellular behavior by regulating intracellular cascades [26: \"acts as a molecular switch to persistently stimulate downstream signaling pathways\"]. The omim title is KRAS PROTOONCOGENE, GTPase; KRAS [4].\n\n## GTPase Regulatory Mechanisms\nThe molecular activation of KRAS is modulated by upstream regulatory proteins [29: \"cognate guanosine exchange factor (GEF) Son of Sevenless 1 that drives KRAS\"]. When structural changes occur, a single residue modification can disrupt this normal regulation [11: \"A single amino acid substitution is responsible for an activating mutation\"]. These structural alterations trap the protein in a continuous active state [26: \"mutations result in constitutive activation of the KRAS protein\"].\n\n## Downstream Signaling Activity\nOnce activated, the KRAS protein propagates signals by engaging subsequent molecular pathways [26: \"persistently stimulate downstream signaling pathways\"]. In certain contexts, its molecular activity involves stimulating specific transcription factors [28: \"activates the transcription factor CP2 (TFCP2)\"]. This transcriptional engagement subsequently increases the cellular production of additional secreted factors [28: \"upregulates the expression of the",
